    "body": "Pacific Fusion said the New Mexico Environment Department (NMED) has approved the company’s radioactive materials license. The move announced November 20 will enable Pacific Fusion to operate a fusion machine and supports the group’s efforts to commercialize fusion energy in the state.\nThe license authorizes Pacific Fusion to possess and use tritium fuel for fusion at the company’s $1-billion Research and Manufacturing Campus in Albuquerque. The move marks the first time state officials have issued a radioactive materials license to a private fusion company.\nThe license approval will keep Pacific Fusion on track toward its goal of net facility gain by 2030, which is when a fusion system generates more energy than what is needed to power the fusion reaction. Pacific Fusion on Friday said the company will continue working closely with NMED as construction progresses.\nCollaboration With State Officials\nThe license approval comes a year after Pacific Fusion announced its decision to build a system in New Mexico. The company said it “reflects close collaboration between the company and state officials to advance a first-of-a-kind energy and advanced manufacturing project while maintaining rigorous safety and environmental standards.”\n“Leaders across the state have demonstrated what’s possible when government and industry work hand in hand to deploy new energy technologies,” said Carrie von Muench, co-founder and COO of Pacific Fusion. “This license is an essential milestone on our path to putting clean fusion energy on the grid, and we’re grateful to the New Mexico Environment Department for their commitment to helping us move quickly and responsibly. We’re proud to be building the future of fusion energy here in New Mexico.”\n“New Mexico is proving that we can lead the clean energy transition while creating good-paying jobs and building the industries of the future,” said New Mexico Environment Secretary James Kenney. “Our Energy Transition Act set ambitious goals for a zero-carbon electricity future, and innovations like fusion could play an important role in helping us achieve them. Today’s milestone is another clear step forward, demonstrating that New Mexico has the talent, infrastructure and commitment to lead the next generation of clean energy innovation while ensuring protections for public health and the environment.”\nEnvironmental Regulation\nFusion is regulated in New Mexico by the state’s Environment Department, under a framework similar to that used for hospitals and research facilities. The small amounts of tritium needed by Pacific Fusion will be contained in a closed system. Any waste will be disposed of at a licensed facility outside the state in a safe manner, according to the company.\nPacific Fusion’s Research and Manufacturing Campus is expected to create more than 200 permanent jobs, along with hundreds of construction jobs. The company said it has expanded its manufacturing operations in Los Lunas, and to-date has hired 90 workers at its Los Lunas Build Center to build the modular components that will power its pulser fusion system.\n“New Mexico has the opportunity to become a national center for America’s fusion energy ecosystem,” von Muench said. “The state’s leadership, scientific expertise and commitment to advanced manufacturing make it an ideal place to build. We’re excited about what this milestone means, not just for Pacific Fusion, but for the jobs, investment and long-term economic opportunity that fusion can bring to New Mexico.”\n— Darrell Proctor is a senior editor for POWER.\nTagged in:\nenvironment\nfusion\nNew Mexico\nnuclear\nPacific Fusion\nradioactive materials license\ntritium",
